{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790329010,"nonce":"6bEibIyEsP2VZ02lkzXvwYg8PlI=","title":"Lambda.has over enum values was never specialized; haxe.macro.Printer.printTypeDefinition could not compile","message":"Lambda.has(it, elt) compares two A values, so its body needs a concrete tag and must be specialized. The monomorphizer infers A only from a primitive argument, and TAST withheld explicit type arguments whenever A appears directly as a parameter; a static extension call (`using Lambda; access.has(AFinal)`) always passed none. Every caller then cascaded into a trap stub, including Printer.printField/printTypeDefinition (Issue3205, 8859, 9565 crashed). Fix: explicit type arguments when a direct argument is not a primitive, and for static extension calls. Those three rows now reach the next gap: runtime `macro class` reification.","files":null}]}